112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 46 0 R
/E (Statiscically, if we look at the ways of the solar energy income Istanbul and Alphen aan den Rijn have a major difference. In Istanbul Solar energy mainly comes from logistic centers and factory roofs while in Alphen aan den Rijn solar energy mainly comes from a households roof. But in Alphen aan den Rijn they also have a few projects which are mainly focused on generating energy for future sale. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 46 0 R
/E (So in  Alphen aan den Rijn people are mainly dependent on their own renewable energy, while in Istanbul, they are mainly dependent on energy which is generated by the government. This difference mainly occurs because the two countries have very a different public policy. For example, in Alphen aan den Rijn the government is more focused on individual investings for solar energy.  While in Istanbul, the government is mainly focused on social investments)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 46 0 R
/E (The two cities both have very prominent plans for the future, but we noticed a few changes which would be worth it. In Alphen aan den Rijn, the government is ofcourse mainly focused on individual solar energy investments but it would be better they also focused a bit more on social investments, this way they can be more prepared for unexpected future events which may involve more energy than planned. In Istanbul, the government could be more focused on individual solar energy investments. The government is ofcourse producing a lot of energy for the entire city but they could produce even more if the solar panels were spread across the city and not just loads on one factory building for example.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
